**命题 11.3（full-real exact and growth limits）。** On the full real class, a source-aware finite word cannot represent every nonzero rotated first-future vector exactly. Nor can one finite word per source answering all tolerances determine every such exact vector. In each growth regime, not even arbitrary individually finite source-dependent words can give a fixed finite whole-future error on the full class. These countability statements do not apply to a merely countable family of sources.

**证明。** Finite words are countable and exact rotated first-future vectors form an uncountable sphere. A word answering every tolerance determines the exact vector as its limit and has the same obstruction. For growth, rotate one actual nonzero growing phase through the sphere; different phase directions have infinite whole-future distance. A word approximating two such sources with finite error would contradict the triangle inequality, so the uncountable family would require uncountably many words. Unlike that valid uncountable obstruction, a countable slow family can assign a different finite code to each member; no code is forced to recur. Fixed positive source-dependent tolerances on plateaus can be included through finite $K$ fields, but their acquisition and representation are paid and no uniform cap for uncharged tolerance information is asserted.

